speechtext:
I should like to get this on the record; it is well worth while:
Whereas an insidious campaign is being carried on, particularly in eastern Canada, with a view to again inflicting upon the people- of British Columbia the Japanese scourge, from which welcome surcease has been given during the past several years;
And whereas paid press propagandists, subversive political groups and ill-advised religious idealists are raising the false issue of racial prejudice in a determined endeavour to prevent the deporation of the Japanese to their homeland and at the same time advocating the restoration of their former privileges and holdings in this province;
And whereas experience has amply demonstrated that the Japanese in Canada are and have been a fascist-minded, easily regimented, closely integrated group of foreigners, wholly unassimilable. and always a threat to our Canadian way of living, and whether Canadian-born, naturalized or not, have always borne true allegiance to the Emperor of Japan only;
And whereas their return to British Columbia will seriously complicate an already difficult situation and render almost impossible the reestablishment of our ex-servicemen in all lines of small business, fishing, farming and fruit growing:
Be it therefore resolved by this Provincial Command of the Army and Navy Veterans in Canada, in convention assembled, that the dominion government be strongly urged to proceed immediately with the repatriation of all persons of Japanese origin; and, in the alternative, should such be impossible, that the lower mainland and the coastal regions of British Columbia be forthwith declared a defence area, from which
all persons of Japanese origin shall be totally excluded for a period of at least ten years next ensuing:
And that copies of this resolution be forwarded to the Right Hon. W. L. Mackenzie King, Prime Minister of Canada, and to the British Columbia members of parliament.

The exemptions are shown in subsection 1 of the new section 3, which says that this act shall not apply to:
(a)	whole hays, straws, corn stover and silage when unmixed with any other material;
(b)	hulled oats, hulled barley, cracked Indian corn and the whole seeds or grains of cultivated farm crops;
(c)	feeding stuff prepared in accordance with a prescription provided and signed by the purchaser for consumption or processing by such purchaser;
(d)	feeding stuff for export from Canada and so labelled;
(e)	feeding stuffs sold by the individual grower thereof.
The act does not apply to them. I shall ask my colleague to move the amendment I mentioned a moment ago.
